                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "235249ba-383e-32c5-9526-ae16ea444270",
                        "entry_id": "bdd19e70-7ac4-3d19-9135-dfe2ee8eb84e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A small, yellow-headed passerine bird, Auriparus flaviceps, endemic to desert areas of the southwestern United States, that is the only species of family Remizidae found in the New World."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ad402ba6-c028-4810-809c-4e591ad4541b",
                                "sense_id": "235249ba-383e-32c5-9526-ae16ea444270",
                                "text": "2000, Kenn Kaufman, Verdin, Steven J. Phillips, Patricia Wentworth Comus (editors), A Natural History of the Sonoran Desert, Arizona-Sonora Desert Museum Press, University of California Press, Revised and Updated Edition, page 422,\nTiny and rather plain, the Verdin seems quite unremarkable at first sight."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9363f99b-c522-4a41-af5f-7bba003a544f",
                                "sense_id": "235249ba-383e-32c5-9526-ae16ea444270",
                                "text": "Verdins are yellow-faced desert-dwellers that are commonly encountered in southern Arizona.[…]Although primarily insectivorous, Verdins will also readily consume nectar, which they obtain by piercing tubular flowers or by visiting hummingbird feeders."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5bc35efb-5924-4336-97a9-f7e98647fdaf",
                                "sense_id": "235249ba-383e-32c5-9526-ae16ea444270",
                                "text": "Desert-dwelling verdins, forage solitarily, in pairs, or in small groups by actively hopping from twig to twig while sounding their surprisingly loud tseet calls almost continuously."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
